{"date": "2026-11-20", "post_id": 1513767, "title": "V-G-R", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-20-v-g-r/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> On November 17, fans of interstellar spaceflight around <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-august-1-tetons-and-snake-river-planet-earth/\">planet Earth</a> were <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/shine-bright-for-voyagers-light-day/\">invited to shine a message</a> into the night sky. Traced by a flashlight beam switched on and off, the dashes and dots of a Morse code message captured in this time exposure from the Emirates Astronomical Observatory, are read V-G-R. That's a call sign for the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/where-are-voyager-1-and-voyager-2-now/\">Voyager 1 spacecraft</a> currently in interstellar space some 26 billion kilometers away. How long did it take the message to reach humanity's most distant <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-september-9-interstellar-voyager/\">interstellar spacecraft</a>? Traveling at the speed of light, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/voyager-1-what-is-a-light-day/\">the answer is one day</a>. In fact, the Voyager 1 spacecraft was confirmed to reach its <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/voyager-1-what-is-a-light-day/#Eyes-On-Light-Day\">one light-day distance milestone</a> on November 18, at 10:16:07 UTC, nearly 50 years after launch in 1977. For extra credit, fans of math can calculate that at one light-day per 50 years, Voyager 1 will reach its one light-year (365 light-days) distance milestone in about 18,250 years.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>pixels in space", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.apostoloskyriazis.com/\">Apostolos Kyriazis</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.apostoloskyriazis.com/\">Apostolos Kyriazis</a>", "alt": "Time exposure of a flashlight beam tracing out Morse code for the letters V - G - R.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-20-v-g-r/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/IMG_4364N_Kyriazis.jpg?w=3898&h=4724&fit=clip", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 20 - V-G-R</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-20-v-g-r/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: On November 17, fans of interstellar spaceflight around planet Earth were invited to shine a message into the night sky. Traced by a flashlight beam switched on and off, the dashes and dots of a Morse code message captured in this time exposure from the Emirates Astronomical Observatory, are read V-G-R. That&#039;s a call sign for the Voyager 1 spacecraft currently in interstellar space some 26 billion kilometers away. How long did it take the message to reach humanity&#039;s most distant interstellar spacecraft? Traveling at the speed of light, the answer is one day. In fact, the Voyager 1 spacecraft was confirmed to reach its one light-day distance milestone on November 18, at 10:16:07 UTC, nearly 50 years after launch in 1977. For extra credit, fans of math can calculate that at one light-day per 50 years, Voyager 1 will reach its one light-year (365 light-days) distance milestone in about 18,250 years.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: pixels in space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"v-g-r\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"V-G-R\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-20-v-g-r/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/IMG_4364N_Kyriazis.jpg?w=3898&#038;h=4724&#038;fit=clip\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/IMG_4364N_Kyriazis.jpg?w=3898&#038;h=4724&#038;fit=clip\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Time exposure of a flashlight beam tracing out Morse code for the letters V - G - R.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-20T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-19T18:53:28-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"V-G-R\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/IMG_4364N_Kyriazis.jpg?w=3898&#038;h=4724&#038;fit=clip\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 20<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/IMG_4364N_Kyriazis.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/IMG_4364N_Kyriazis.jpg\" alt=\"Time exposure of a flashlight beam tracing out Morse code for the letters V - G - R.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> V-G-R </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.apostoloskyriazis.com/\">Apostolos Kyriazis</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> On November 17, fans of interstellar spaceflight around <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-august-1-tetons-and-snake-river-planet-earth/\">planet Earth</a> were <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/shine-bright-for-voyagers-light-day/\">invited to shine a message</a> into the night sky. Traced by a flashlight beam switched on and off, the dashes and dots of a Morse code message captured in this time exposure from the Emirates Astronomical Observatory, are read V-G-R. That's a call sign for the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/where-are-voyager-1-and-voyager-2-now/\">Voyager 1 spacecraft</a> currently in interstellar space some 26 billion kilometers away. How long did it take the message to reach humanity's most distant <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-september-9-interstellar-voyager/\">interstellar spacecraft</a>? Traveling at the speed of light, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/voyager-1-what-is-a-light-day/\">the answer is one day</a>. In fact, the Voyager 1 spacecraft was confirmed to reach its <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/voyager-1-what-is-a-light-day/#Eyes-On-Light-Day\">one light-day distance milestone</a> on November 18, at 10:16:07 UTC, nearly 50 years after launch in 1977. For extra credit, fans of math can calculate that at one light-day per 50 years, Voyager 1 will reach its one light-year (365 light-days) distance milestone in about 18,250 years.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>pixels in space\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-19-the-horsehead-and-flame-nebulas/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nJerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261120/html"}